+# AA77 air-traffic-control source and clock-record leads
+
+October 27, 2026. Original-record work plan, recorder track; AUD016-A1 / TC01–TC04. This is a source-acquisition and document-comparison result. No new recorder decoding, audio inspection, clock fit or impact-time measurement was performed.
+
+## Finding and significance
+
+The acquired December 21, 2001 NTSB **Air Traffic Control Recording** specialist's report identifies a concrete clock-verification method: marked transcript times were verified through an **FAA timecode reader**. It also says the times are approximate and describes a composite assembled from selected portions of analog recordings. The reader output, its accuracy, and the tape-to-composite mapping are therefore specific missing inputs for independent timing tests. A timestamp printed to the second is not, by itself, proof of one-second accuracy.
+
+The file preserves differences between its source-period table and transcript labels, and between its stated composite endpoint and a later transcript entry. These observations do not establish editing intended to deceive, a missing interval in a recovered WAV, a fabricated flight, or a conspiracy. Editorial errors and different source/selection conventions are untested alternatives. Neither alternative is adopted as the explanation.
+
+## Source, acquisition and reading scope
+
+The [National Security Archive's briefing book 196](https://nsarchive2.gwu.edu/NSAEBB/NSAEBB196/), posted August 11, 2006, links Document 6 to the [13-page report](https://nsarchive2.gwu.edu/NSAEBB/NSAEBB196/doc06.pdf). Its account says the NTSB supplied the documents. That account is not an acquired agency release letter, original certification or independent authentication of the current hosted bytes. The article's separate claim that Flight 93 had the only surviving recorder is not adopted: the previously held official AA77 FDR report documents another recovered recorder. We use the article as navigation and distinguish its commentary from the linked report.
+
+The complete current PDF was acquired October 27, 2026, HTTP 200, `application/pdf`, **383,550 bytes**, SHA-256 **`74e5acd39a081746cf464f15971e067e7b9173abace7a3a3be9779b3f91c456d`**. [Preserved bytes](sources/gwu-document06-AA77-atc-report.pdf); [source pins](SOURCES.json). The hash identifies this acquisition, not its 2001 creation history or its 2006 byte identity. The scan has no extracted text and no embedded names found through pypdf's attachment interface. We did not obtain a hidden/native audio attachment.
+
+All 13 physical pages were rendered and visually inspected individually. The first three are introductory pages; physical 4–11 contain the numbered eight-page transcript; physical 12–13 are unnumbered monospaced transcript pages repeating entries. Their appended provenance is not separately established, and the two layouts are not independent witnesses. No exhaustive character-level comparison of the layouts was performed. Tesseract OCR was run for all pages, with separate clockwise-rotated derivatives for pages 4–11; raw OCR is only a locator. One control shows OCR reading `1231:24` where the scan shows `1231:21`. That is our extraction error, not a source discrepancy. [Visually checked readings R01–R12](VERIFIED-READINGS.md) and their PDF page locations control the quoted observations.
+
+The previously acquired [January 31, 2002 official AA77 FDR report](https://www.ntsb.gov/about/Documents/AAL77_fdr.pdf) was not redownloaded. Its hash was rechecked and physical pages 1 and 3 were viewed again. It remains a separate source, with SHA-256 `5784213460f2a2e422f853547611c62f1c71ccae50103f9ffc83a5cae40430cc`.
+
